    "result_text": "There's only one test class, and it passes. The build is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the tenure-based membership level rule so that **level 4 requires tenure of more than 365 days**.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`Owner.membershipLevel(...)`** (`src/main/java/.../model/Owner.java`) \u2014 changed the signature from `(Integer namesakeCount, String email)` to `(Integer namesakeCount, String email, LocalDate registrationDate)`. The level still starts at 1 and gains +1 for a present email and +1 for `namesakeCount == 0`, but the previous hard `Math.min(level, 3)` cap (with a comment that \"level 4 is reserved for tenure\") is replaced by a real tenure factor: +1 when the days from `registrationDate` to today exceed 365, capped at 4. Added the `java.time.temporal.ChronoUnit` import and updated the Javadoc.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.formatMembershipLevel(...)`** (`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`) \u2014 now passes `owner.getRegistrationDate()` through to the model method, and its Javadoc/`@link` were updated to match the new signature.\n\nBecause a newly created owner's `registrationDate` is set to today (zero tenure), a new owner with an email and `namesakeCount` of 0 maxes the pre-tenure factors at **level 3, never 4** \u2014 exactly as the acceptance test `coreNewOwnerCappedAtLevel3` asserts.\n\nThe full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test, 0 failures).",
